-- title:
--   Kernel degree of the outer factor divides that of a composite
-- statement:
--   Let $B$ be a non-zero Noetherian commutative ring and let $\varphi,\psi$ each be a pair of formal power series in two variables over $B$ (elements of `Series B`, i.e. functions $\mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to B[[X_0,X_1]]$) whose components all have vanishing constant term. Write $\mathrm{KerAlgebra}\,\theta$ for the quotient of $B[[X_0,X_1]]$ by the ideal generated by the two components of $\theta$, and say that $\theta$ has kernel of degree $d$ when this quotient is finite and projective as a $B$-module and, for every field $\kappa$ and every ring homomorphism $f : B \to \kappa$, the quotient of $\kappa[[X_0,X_1]]$ by the ideal generated by the components of $\theta$ with coefficients pushed along $f$ has $\kappa$-dimension exactly $d$. Assume that $\psi$ has kernel of degree $e$ and that the composite $\psi \circ \varphi$, whose $i$-th component is the substitution of $\varphi$ into $\psi_i$, has kernel of degree $D$. Then $e$ divides $D$. Nothing is assumed about $\varphi$ beyond the vanishing of its constant terms; in particular $\varphi$ is not assumed to have a kernel of finite degree.
--
--   This is the divisibility half of the multiplicativity of degrees of isogenies of two-dimensional formal groups with $\mathcal{O}_D$-action: the degree of the outer factor divides the degree of the composite. It feeds the rigidification step for fake elliptic curves in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation, where an identity between composites of isogenies and multiplication-by-$p^a$ maps is turned into a divisibility of degrees before cancellation.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CerednikDrinfeld_FormalODModule_HasKernelOfDegree_dvd_of_comp.lean

